1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,088
What should a merchant's server log in with?

2
00:00:02,138 --> 00:00:05,616
In Lesson 18, two admins switched the merchant on.

3
00:00:05,666 --> 00:00:10,080
In Lesson 1, the shop's server asked ecom to create each payment.

4
00:00:10,130 --> 00:00:14,352
This lesson gives that server its own login, separate from any person's.

5
00:00:14,402 --> 00:00:19,992
Picture a merchant's developer who is told to put the owner's portal password into the server's settings.

6
00:00:20,042 --> 00:00:22,896
The password ends up in a shared code store.

7
00:00:22,946 --> 00:00:26,808
When the developer leaves, the owner's own login has to change.

8
00:00:26,858 --> 00:00:31,248
And every request the server ever made looks like the owner clicking buttons.

9
00:00:31,298 --> 00:00:33,672
You never give a cleaner your own house keys.

10
00:00:33,722 --> 00:00:37,776
You give them a key of their own, which you can change without changing yours.

11
00:00:37,826 --> 00:00:39,912
A merchant's server gets the same.

12
00:00:39,962 --> 00:00:41,784
Here is how it works.

13
00:00:41,834 --> 00:00:48,312
An active merchant creates an API client: a login for a machine, not for a person.

14
00:00:48,362 --> 00:00:54,912
It has an id, like a username, and a client secret, like a password that only the machine knows.

15
00:00:54,962 --> 00:00:58,776
The secret is shown exactly once, when the client is made.

16
00:00:58,826 --> 00:01:02,160
ecom keeps the client's id, but never the secret.

17
00:01:02,210 --> 00:01:07,296
So nobody at the bank, and nobody who breaks into ecom's database, can read it back.

18
00:01:07,346 --> 00:01:11,688
The merchant's server sends its id and secret to the login server.

19
00:01:11,738 --> 00:01:15,648
It gets back a short-lived token: a pass that expires soon.

20
00:01:15,698 --> 00:01:18,504
Only that token reaches the payments service.

21
00:01:18,554 --> 00:01:22,440
And only a machine login with the merchant's role can reach it at all.

22
00:01:22,490 --> 00:01:24,312
People's logins cannot.

23
00:01:24,362 --> 00:01:30,240
When a secret may have leaked, or the developer who knew it leaves, the merchant rotates it.

24
00:01:30,290 --> 00:01:36,024
That is rotation: a new secret is issued, and the old one stops working at once.

25
00:01:36,074 --> 00:01:38,472
The owner's own login is never touched.

26
00:01:38,522 --> 00:01:43,176
Only an active merchant can create or rotate an API client.

27
00:01:43,226 --> 00:01:45,048
Now watch the product.

28
00:01:45,098 --> 00:01:49,800
First, a merchant that is not yet switched on opens API clients.

29
00:01:49,850 --> 00:01:51,672
There is no button at all.

30
00:01:51,722 --> 00:01:59,616
The page says API clients open when you are live", and explains that two bank admins must activate the account first.

31
00:01:59,666 --> 00:02:02,064
Now the same page for an active merchant.

32
00:02:02,114 --> 00:02:06,528
The page explains that the merchant's servers use these to get access tokens.

33
00:02:06,578 --> 00:02:08,640
The viewer clicks New client.

34
00:02:08,690 --> 00:02:12,144
A window opens: "Your new API client".

35
00:02:12,194 --> 00:02:15,096
It shows the client id and the client secret.

36
00:02:15,146 --> 00:02:21,984
Above them is a warning: this is the only time you will see this secret, so store it in your server's secret manager now.

37
00:02:22,034 --> 00:02:26,472
The viewer copies both, and ticks "I have stored the secret safely".

38
00:02:26,522 --> 00:02:32,400
The window closes, and the list shows the new client with its id and the date it was created.

39
00:02:32,450 --> 00:02:36,168
The secret is nowhere on the page, and it never will be again.

40
00:02:36,218 --> 00:02:41,856
The viewer's server now uses the id and secret to get a token, and the token works.

41
00:02:41,906 --> 00:02:44,688
Then the viewer clicks Rotate secret.

42
00:02:44,738 --> 00:02:51,048
The portal asks first: the current secret stops working immediately, so update your servers right after.

43
00:02:51,098 --> 00:02:52,920
The viewer confirms.

44
00:02:52,970 --> 00:02:56,568
A new secret appears, once, with the same warning.

45
00:02:56,618 --> 00:03:00,912
The server tries the old secret again, and the login server refuses it.

46
00:03:00,962 --> 00:03:02,784
What does this protect?

47
00:03:02,834 --> 00:03:08,568
A person's password in a server's settings leaks, and its requests cannot be told apart from the person.

48
00:03:08,618 --> 00:03:12,264
A separate key for each server keeps the owner's login private.

49
00:03:12,314 --> 00:03:17,688
It lets the merchant cut off one leaked key in one click, without locking anyone out.

50
00:03:17,738 --> 00:03:22,680
And because ecom never keeps the secret, the bank cannot leak it either.

51
00:03:22,730 --> 00:03:28,152
So, like the cleaner's own key: changed in one click, and the owner's key never touched.

52
00:03:28,202 --> 00:03:30,480
Next: the people on the merchant's team.
